# Forward a failing CI check to the owning worker (candidate 1)

## Premise check

**Premise 1: "the dispatcher's cache must remember something so a failure is forwarded once."** Yes, but much less than the question implies. If we built this from scratch with every signal now available, the only state needed would be a set of check identities already typed into the worker. "Pending" state would not exist. The comment nudge needs a watermark (`CommentSeen`) and a hold-back set (`PendingComments`) because Linear comments are a stream: once the watermark moves past a comment, nothing brings it back. A failing check is different. It stays visible on GitHub (`statusCheckRollup.contexts` of the head commit) until a re-run or a push replaces it. So "owed" can be derived on every tick as *failing on the current head minus already forwarded*. Blocked worker, failed `herdr.Prompt`, gh error, comment nudge taking the tick: all of these fall out as "not recorded yet, still failing, so it goes on a later tick", and none needs its own field. This design therefore adds **one** cache field and **no** pending flag. A design that copied the `CommentSeen`/`PendingComments` pattern would build machinery the data source makes unnecessary.

**Premise 2: "the existing PR nudge is the natural home."** No. From scratch, this would not be a branch of `inReviewIdle`. The evidence: `inReviewIdle` (reconcile.go:374) runs only for `worker.Idle` on In Review. It fires on *state* every tick until `NudgeLimit`, and it counts toward escalation (`Nudges++` in the executor, dispatch.go:423). The forward has to reach working workers and In Progress tickets, fire on an *event* once per run, and stay out of the escalation count. Those are a different trigger, different eligible states and a different counter. The only shared parts are the transport (`Nudge` action, `herdr.Prompt`) and the PR read. This makes it a separate rule that sits next to `commentNudge`, which is the existing rule with the same profile: event-triggered, working-or-idle, uncounted, takes the tick first (reconcile.go:339).

**Premise 3: "a tick-time poll is the signal."** Yes, it would exist from scratch. A GitHub webhook or check-suite subscription would need an endpoint that GitHub can reach. The dispatcher is a foreground CLI on one laptop (blueprint "One machine runs everything"), with no server, and workers may not poll (SKILL.md line 10, blueprint invariant 6). The tick is the only clock. What changes is *what* the poll reads: per-check data in the same single GraphQL call, at no extra calls per ticket read.

**Premise 4 (implicit): "per check run" means GitHub's check run id.** That holds, and keying by head would be wrong. Step 7 of the build skill tells the worker to *re-run* a flaky check (SKILL.md:29). A re-run on the same head creates a new check run. If it fails again, the worker has to hear about it, and it can't poll. A head-keyed marker would swallow that second failure. So identity is the run id for CheckRuns, and (context, createdAt) for legacy StatusContexts.

## Problem

When CI fails on a ticket's PR, the worker only finds out if the user relays it, or after the In Review worker goes idle and the state-based PR nudge says "has a failed check" without naming which one. A working worker or an In Progress ticket never hears about it. Constraints that shape the design:
- `pullrequest.Read` returns only rollup state, with no per-check identity (pullrequest.go:58, 134).
- `Plan` is pure. "Forwarded" can only be written by the executor after `herdr.Prompt` succeeds (dispatch.go:415).
- `settle` runs before actions (dispatch.go:366).
- Nudge texts are fixed and asserted exactly by tests.
- The cache must stay disposable (cache.go:2–5, blueprint line 59).
- PR reads are the main per-tick cost (blueprint line 300).

## Usage (caller's view)

The surface is the tick. What the user and the worker see:

```
$ dispatch tick --dry-run
nudge ENG-301: check failed: test
nudge ENG-288: new comment
nudge ENG-290: PR needs work: a failed check
```

Text typed into ENG-301's worker, which is working mid-turn on an In Progress ticket with a draft PR:

```
Checks failed on PR https://github.com/kapetan-io/dispatch/pull/41 for ENG-301: test (https://github.com/kapetan-io/dispatch/actions/runs/9/job/77). Read each failure's log, handle it as your build skill's step 7 says, and push.
```

Call sites in the code:

```go
// reconcile.watch: event rules first, comment before check.
if a, ok := in.commentNudge(t, w, prev); ok { return a, true }
if a, ok := in.checkNudge(t, w, prev); ok { return a, true }

// dispatch.go executor, Nudge case after a successful Prompt:
switch a.Reason {
case reconcile.ReasonComment:
    e.CommentSeen = a.Ticket.LastComment
case reconcile.ReasonCheck:
    e.Forwarded = e.Forwarded.With(a.Checks)
default:
    e.Nudges++
    e.ResumePending = false
}

// dispatch.go settle, for any ticket whose PR was read this tick:
e.Forwarded = e.Forwarded.Keep(pr.FailedIDs())
```

## Shape

### Data: the GitHub read (`internal/pullrequest`)

The same single `gh api graphql` call. The query grows inside `statusCheckRollup`:

```graphql
statusCheckRollup { state contexts(first: 100) {
  pageInfo { hasNextPage }
  nodes { __typename
    ... on CheckRun { databaseId name conclusion detailsUrl }
    ... on StatusContext { context state targetUrl createdAt } } } }
```

```go
// CheckID identifies one failed run of one check. It is unique across reruns:
// a re-run makes a new id. Only this package builds one, so callers can't
// forge or mis-key it (per encode-lessons-in-structure).
type CheckID struct{ v string } // "run:<databaseId>" | "status:<context>@<createdAt>"

func (c CheckID) String() string { return c.v }
// MarshalText/UnmarshalText so cache.json stores it as a plain string.
func (c CheckID) MarshalText() ([]byte, error)  { /* TODO */ }
func (c *CheckID) UnmarshalText(b []byte) error { /* TODO */ }

// FailedCheck is one failed check on the PR's head commit, in domain terms.
// The wire's __typename/conclusion/state never leave this package (per boundary-discipline).
type FailedCheck struct {
    ID   CheckID
    Name string // CheckRun.name or StatusContext.context
    URL  string // detailsUrl or targetUrl; may be ""
}

type PR struct {
    Number int
    URL    string
    Head   string
    // Failed lists the failed checks on Head, in the order GitHub returns them.
    // A CheckRun fails on conclusion FAILURE, TIMED_OUT, STARTUP_FAILURE or
    // ACTION_REQUIRED. A StatusContext fails on state FAILURE or ERROR.
    // CANCELLED is not a failure (see Open questions).
    Failed   []FailedCheck
    Problems []string // "a failed check" now derives from len(Failed) > 0
    Ready    bool
}

// FailedIDs is the set of Failed ids. settle prunes the cache against it.
func (p PR) FailedIDs() map[CheckID]bool { /* TODO */ }

// Read: unchanged signature. New error when contexts.pageInfo.hasNextPage is
// true, the same policy as review threads: a failure past page one would go unforwarded.
func Read(ctx context.Context, bin, repoDir, branch string, timeout time.Duration) (PR, bool, error)
```

There is one source of truth for "a failed check". Today `Problems` gets it from rollup state. After this change it derives from `Failed`, so the PR nudge and the forward can't disagree about whether a check failed. Rollup `state` is still read for `Ready`, since `checksPassed` also needs PENDING and EXPECTED.

### Data: the cache (`internal/cache`)

```go
// Forwarded is the set of failed checks on the ticket's PR that a worker has
// already been told about. It holds only ids still failing at the last PR
// read, so it stays as small as the PR's failing checks.
type Forwarded []pullrequest.CheckID // json: "forwarded_checks", omitempty

func (f Forwarded) Has(id pullrequest.CheckID) bool            { /* TODO */ }
// With returns f plus ids, without duplicates. Idempotent: a run that is
// recorded twice stays recorded once (per make-operations-idempotent).
func (f Forwarded) With(ids []pullrequest.CheckID) Forwarded   { /* TODO */ }
// Keep returns the ids of f that are in failing. A fixed or re-run check drops
// out, and since ids are never reused, a dropped one can't come back.
func (f Forwarded) Keep(failing map[pullrequest.CheckID]bool) Forwarded { /* TODO */ }

type Entry struct {
    // ...existing fields...
    Forwarded Forwarded `json:"forwarded_checks,omitempty"`
}
```

`cache` already sits below `reconcile`. It does not import `pullrequest` today. Importing it for `CheckID` is a leaf-to-leaf import with no cycle (`pullrequest` imports only the stdlib).

**Lifetime of `Forwarded`:**

| Event | Effect | Where |
|---|---|---|
| Prompt succeeds for a `ReasonCheck` nudge | `Forwarded = Forwarded.With(a.Checks)` | executor, the only writer |
| PR read this tick (any status) | `Forwarded = Forwarded.Keep(pr.FailedIDs())` | `settle` |
| PR not read (worker blocked, gh error, ticket in Needs Human) | untouched | `settle` |
| Ticket absent from Linear read, tree leased | kept (next to `ReadyHead`) | `settle` kept branch |
| Ticket absent, no lease | dropped | `settle` kept branch |
| Status change | **not** cleared | (ids are unique, so a stale id is harmless and the next read prunes it) |

**After the cache is deleted:** every check still failing on a PR read next tick is forwarded once more. This is deliberate, and it is the opposite of `CommentSeen`'s "first sighting nudges nobody". An old comment may already have been handled. A check failing on the *current head* is always still unhandled, because handling it means a push or a re-run, and either one removes it from `Failed`. The cost is at most one repeated message per failing check. Swallowing it instead would leave a working In Progress worker with no backstop, because the PR nudge never reaches In Progress.

### Rule: `checkNudge` (`internal/reconcile`)

```go
