# Forward a failing CI check to the worker that owns the pull request

Candidate 2. Everything below was traced from code and docs; nothing was run against live GitHub.

## Premise check

The question assumes four things. Each is tested against the code, not named and waved through.

1. **The dispatcher must remember something.** Could "already told" be derived instead of stored? The only observable worker signals are herdr's `working|idle|blocked` (`internal/worker/worker.go`) and the PR head (`PRHead`). Neither says "the worker has heard about run 123". A head move proves a push, not that the push was a response to a given run, and a re-run on the same head creates a new run with no head change. GitHub keeps no "delivered to worker" bit. So with polling, a stored identity is forced. The cache is the only cross-tick memory the dispatcher has, and its disposability is a stated invariant (`cache.go` package doc; blueprint invariant "Deleting the dispatcher cache loses little"), so the field must be safe to lose. It is (see Shape).

2. **A tick-time poll is the signal.** From scratch, a `check_run.completed` webhook carries the run id and would need no diff against a cache. But the dispatcher is a foreground process on one workstation with no inbound endpoint, and decision 11 (`docs/design/decision-log.md` line 97) deliberately made "poll each source, compare with the cache, forward what the receiver doesn't know" the one shape for every event kind. A webhook receiver for one event would be a second event shape next to the comment rule. So in this system the poll-and-diff mechanism would exist from scratch. Yes.

3. **The existing PR nudge is the natural home.** No. `inReviewIdle` (`reconcile.go` 374-387) fires on state ("the PR currently needs work"), repeats every tick, counts toward `NudgeLimit`, reads only idle or gone In Review workers (`ReadsPR`, line 353), and its text is a fixed phrase the build skill keys on. The feature needs an event trigger (a run that failed since last told), delivery to a working worker and an In Progress ticket, and no escalation count. Those are the comment rule's properties (`commentNudge`, line 339; decision 11 bullet "counts toward neither the stall nor the PR limit"). The comment rule is the template, and decision 11 says so in words: "A new kind of event is a new forwarding rule in the dispatcher."

4. **The worker needs telling at all.** An idle In Review worker already gets "PR has a failed check" within one tick of going idle. The gap is the working worker (never read today, so a failure that lands while it is busy on review comments waits until it goes idle, then is reported without a name or log URL) and the In Progress ticket whose PR Linear bounced back. Blueprint invariant 6 forbids the worker watching checks itself. So yes.

Verdict: build it, as a new forwarding rule with one new cache field, not as a change to the PR nudge.

## Problem

When a check fails on the PR of an In Progress or In Review ticket, the owning worker should hear which run failed, once, without the user relaying anything, even while it is working. Today the PR is read only for idle or gone In Review workers, the GraphQL query returns only `statusCheckRollup.state` (no run identity, so two failures on one head are indistinguishable), and the one nudge that mentions checks is state-triggered and capped. Constraints the design honors: `Plan` is pure and runs after `settle` rolls the cache forward, so a delivery marker can only be written by the executor after `herdr.Prompt` succeeds (`dispatch.go` 411-427); a blocked worker cannot take text (herdr refuses, and the comment path holds its marker back for the same reason); fixed nudge text is relied on by the build skill and tests; the cache must stay disposable; entries for absent tickets are pruned to a kept set, with `CommentSeen`/`ReadyHead` surviving only while a tree is leased.

## Usage (caller's view)

The consumer is the tick in `dispatch.go` and the surface tests that drive it. From the user's seat nothing is configured; a failing check on a managed PR shows up in the worker's pane as:

```
PR https://github.com/kapetan-io/dispatch/pull/7 for ENG-1 has a failed check, test (https://github.com/kapetan-io/dispatch/actions/runs/42/job/9). Handle it as your build skill's step 7 says, and push.
```

Two runs failing together become one message: `has 2 failed checks, lint (url) and test (url)`. The same run is never named twice. A dry run prints `forward ENG-1: failed check test`.

Call site 1, the tick (unchanged shape; `readPRs` already loops `ReadsPR`):

```go
